The puns would really be a problem. In worst cases there could be an
explanation somewhere (a separate Translation-FAQ in the documentation,
for example) that would tell the player what this thing was all about.
It's akward, but I think it's the same problem in all translations of
fiction, be it a book or a movie or a game.

What would be a problem too is that many of the monsters don't exist in
other languages (as Joe said about fire elementals and such). I think I
have written about this issue sometime in this group (or r.g.r.misc).
That's a real challenge for even a professional translator. I heard a
radio interview of the finnish translators who translated Lord of the
rings back in the 50's and they actually created the word "=F6rkki" (orc
in finnish) which has become quite a commonly used word also outside of
LOTR context. So it can be done but you'll propably have to create a
few new words altogether. Some words that don't exist in finnish (and I
doubt they would in chinese) include for example wigth, wraith,
cockatrice.. Also there are a few quite misleading similarities in some
words - for example a Troll is a very similar with "trull" or "trulli"
which is a perhaps swedish word borrowed to finnish and means something
similar to a Witch. So I was quite confused when I started to play
nethack :). And also the semi-official translation of a troll -
"peikko" - sounds much smaller and less fearsome creature than the
nethack troll. This all, I believe, comes from differrences in
differrent countries folklores. Luckily there are quite many names that
don't need to be translated - Excalibur, Uruk-hai and some that should
be easy to translate - Magicbane, firebrand.

